WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.839
Level one left us with a gauge that has a size. Ask how

2
00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:09.212
it was measured and there are two different questions hiding in the answer.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.272
Here is one part, and three operators who

4
00:00:12.372 --> 00:00:15.344
are each going to measure it three times.

5
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:20.100
Operator A goes first. Three readings of one part,

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.851
and they do not agree. That disagreement is repeatability

7
00:00:24.951 --> 00:00:28.269
- one person, one part, one gauge, again.

8
00:00:28.366 --> 00:00:32.226
Call it the width of one operator's own answer.

9
00:00:32.316 --> 00:00:36.128
Now hand the same part to two other operators.

10
00:00:36.216 --> 00:00:41.063
Each operator has an average, and the averages are not the same.

11
00:00:41.163 --> 00:00:46.628
That is a second distance, and it is a different thing: reproducibility.

12
00:00:46.716 --> 00:00:50.648
One word, R and R, covers both. They are not the

13
00:00:50.748 --> 00:00:54.344
same size and they do not have the same fix.

14
00:00:56.033 --> 00:01:00.475
Whether that second distance is real depends on one thing:

15
00:01:00.575 --> 00:01:04.861
does it persist? Measure every part with every operator.

16
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.570
Operator A reads low on every part.

17
00:01:08.666 --> 00:01:10.606
B reads higher.

18
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.799
C sits close to B. The gaps hold across

19
00:01:13.899 --> 00:01:17.656
all ten parts, so they belong to the operators.

20
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.334
Roughly parallel lines. An offset that travels with the person

21
00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:27.093
is reproducibility; if these lines crossed each other the story

22
00:01:27.193 --> 00:01:30.418
would be different, and that is level three.

23
00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:34.884
Level one called the whole thing the gauge term.

24
00:01:34.983 --> 00:01:37.649
It splits the same way everything

25
00:01:37.749 --> 00:01:40.835
in this subject splits. Variances add.

26
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:45.273
Now improve one of them and watch what the gauge does.

27
00:01:45.366 --> 00:01:50.572
Halve the repeatability and the gauge improves by nine percent. Halve

28
00:01:50.672 --> 00:01:56.109
the reproducibility instead and it improves by thirty five. Same effort,

29
00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:00.800
nearly four times the return, and the only thing that decided

30
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.106
it was which term was bigger to begin with.

31
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.068
Which is why telling somebody to fix the gauge is not advice.

32
00:02:10.866 --> 00:02:14.807
There is a trap in measuring the operator term. Each

33
00:02:14.907 --> 00:02:19.392
operator's average is itself made of noisy readings, so the

34
00:02:19.492 --> 00:02:23.822
spread of those averages carries repeatability inside it.

35
00:02:23.916 --> 00:02:28.725
On a gauge whose repeatability is large, this matters a lot.

36
00:02:28.825 --> 00:02:33.224
The real operator term here is four tenths of a micron.

37
00:02:33.316 --> 00:02:37.985
With only a handful of readings behind each average, the spread

38
00:02:38.085 --> 00:02:42.301
of the averages is nearly double the truth. Almost all of

39
00:02:42.401 --> 00:02:46.464
what it is reporting is the instrument, not the people.

40
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:51.209
Grow the study and the borrowed part shrinks, because it enters

41
00:02:51.309 --> 00:02:55.666
divided by the number of readings. It never quite vanishes.

42
00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.834
So the honest estimate subtracts the borrowed term. That subtraction

43
00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:04.493
is not a refinement. On this gauge two thirds of

44
00:03:04.593 --> 00:03:08.610
the naive number was never about the operators at all.

45
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.142
Subtracting one estimate from another has a consequence. The

46
00:03:15.242 --> 00:03:18.532
answer is not guaranteed to be a variance.

47
00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:23.053
Run study after study on that same noisy gauge. Every

48
00:03:23.153 --> 00:03:28.275
time the subtraction lands below zero, the result is reported

49
00:03:28.375 --> 00:03:31.356
as zero - no operator effect at all.

50
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.626
On this gauge it happens about 47 percent of the time.

51
00:03:35.726 --> 00:03:39.824
And because zero is a floor and not a correction, the

52
00:03:39.924 --> 00:03:44.576
reported number now runs low - the uncorrected one ran high.

53
00:03:44.676 --> 00:03:47.982
The same study is wrong in both directions.

54
00:03:48.066 --> 00:03:52.574
Three operators give 2 degrees of freedom on the operator term,

55
00:03:52.674 --> 00:03:56.598
which is about fifty percent of error. So we have split

56
00:03:56.698 --> 00:04:01.352
the gauge, and we still cannot see whether the operators disagree

57
00:04:01.452 --> 00:04:04.790
about particular parts. That is the next level.

